and in our book TV bus is John's secret  young his book is called remains non  viewable a memoir what is it about well  it's it's about a in one sense a New  England family and my cousin Doug I was  one of 22 cousins just on that one side  of the family my dad was one of 10 and  he went off to Vietnam and was killed  and so some of the stories about waiting  for him to come back and the family  gathering and some of course is about  growing up in New England and the sort  of lessons of fathers and sons and how  in New England of course taciturnity is  is the goal I mean silence is nirvana  so in our family this event happened but  though Doug when bodies came back from  Vietnam there were sort of two  categories remains viewable or remains  non viewable Doug's body was actually  viewable but the subject matter within  the family once it happened was non  viewable it was something had happened  you don't talk about it basically the  assumption is there are no consequences  or aftermath or after-effects and  metaphorically I think that was true  about Vietnam itself we didn't want to  think about it talk about a deal with it  learn from it all of which is you know  not what happens after a war whether  it's a quote good war or bad war it has  impact on those who served and those in  the families of those who served how old  were you when Doug was killed I was uh  22 and under what circumstances did did  he die well you know the family you  first almost nothing  eventually we heard that the wonderful  phrase friendly-fire I did not know  actually into the book was published  I've learned more since from people who  have called me who were there when Doug  was there and said that he's you know  the the awful circumstances where it  wasn't that he was shot by another  American as he was apparently the  American Patrol walked into a trap the  South Vietnamese had said and they fired  on the Americans who ostensibly were  their allies but you know it's it's very  much a tale of a you know  he was what he went in was like 19 and a  half or 20 years old and there certainly  was looking for adventure and in his  innocence you know I sometimes think  it's like Huck Finn getting on the wrong  Mississippi you know looking for  adventure in the wrong places and you  know the book starts with a question of  sort of what happened over there how was  he killed and in a way I'm not sure you  ever find the exact answer or enough of  an answer to that and then it course  becomes a question of will what who was  he when he left who was the boy man that  I knew you know what was our family what  lessons were we taught by our dads and I  suppose in some way what how did that  help for me and turn me into a writer  that subject which has haunted me but  obviously not me alone  did you feel that that the New England  part of this was really the key to the  silence or have you found out have you  found that the silence among families of  Vietnam participants who died is  universal well I think New England has a  unique quality of silence but there is  no doubt that when men serve and they  come back many of them do not talk about  their experiences you know I did a show  for four years called China Beach and we  found the letters that we got were not  only about can I have an autographed  picture of Dana Delany or marg  Helgenberger who were in the show was  often letters from the wives of the sons  and daughters of you know the mothers  our fathers of veterans who were saying  our son or daughter did not talk about  their experiences all until maybe they  were sitting beside us in an episode of  your show was on the air and then they  started to talk you know a couple of  years ago bill Broyles who created the  show with me and I spoke at the Lyndon  Johnson Library in Austin Texas and we  were amazed at how we were then sort of  confronted by people who were saying  that just now and this is like 2005 that  their family members were sometimes just  talking about what had happened to them  but wasn't that true of the generation  before of the men and women who were  coming back from World War two well  you know war has a tendency to either  the experiences are so primal you know  if you survive you know you are lucky  you know there isn't justice and who  lives and who dies it isn't based on  good or bad your best friend can be next  to you one moment and be you know mush  the next and when you come back this  sort of the tous the two solutions are I  can't talk about it I don't know how to  deal with it I don't how to express it  or I turn it into an anecdote you know  God knows the Armed Forces whether it's  about paperwork or whether it's about  the food I mean you turn it into yarn or  anecdote or joke or laughter to try and  protect those deeper things that you  carry with inside you you talk about the  impact of the people around the person  who served and who died what was the  impact on your family and on Doug's  family what were the repercussions well  I think again the New England component  is that this that we seem to  successfully bury that for so long I  think the ramifications are that you  know in any family it just isn't right  when your son or daughter dies before  you do so I think it through the next  generation off kilter it's not as if  they all went on benders my dad was an  alcoholic I don't think that's the  reason I think it may have fed one of  the many ingredients that may have fed  it but you know it it played out in a  way where I think it led not to the  dissolution of family members but a sort  of disconnection an inability to  communicate only furthered by what  happened you know and in some cases it's  only now that we're starting to say  let's talk about it or let's just sort  of reach across that and recognize how  emotional it was and how it still is  emotional right on the surface if you  start to talk about it or deal with it  had you thought this completely through  before China Beach no not at all I think  that for years I wrote around the truth  you know I wrote a miniseries based on a  book by a man named Philip Caputo called  a rumor of war I wrote you know and  create a co-creator with Bill China  Beach and then I tried to do a mini  serie huge miniseries about sort of the  American experience in Vietnam which  probably not surprisingly never got made  and I think once in a way that my dad  and my uncle I was also close to Doug's  father died it allowed me to say now is  the time to think about who were they  what was that Greatest Generation  because so many of them served as well  and what were the lessons sometimes  explicit and sometimes implicit of what  it was to be a man that that we had been  left with and that you know that I carry  with me still and of course now that I  have children you start to realize oh my  god I'm passing on these lessons  sometimes knowingly sometimes not to you  know my own sons and daughters so from  China Beach you also went did the the  other side of it you were working on the  human side of it and in this book to the  West Wing which is pretty far removed  from the day-to-day the day-to-day life  of someone who might have lost their  life and or what was that like well I  think one of the things is you know I  also did a miniseries about the Gulf War  for summary or the guys coming home and  hey you know there's no such thing quote  unquote as Gulf War Syndrome but why are  all these men and women have these  mysterious illnesses well some of it  people stay sometimes it's psychological  well does that mean it's not an illness  because there are ramifications of  watching you know horrible things happen  around you well isn't that human indeed  to have to deal to have to kind of  wrestle with that inside yourself and  West Wing I think what it offered was  you know one very very smart show I came  in not at the beginning I came on to a  sort of a moving train but you know job  was I was a writer producer on it and  sort of came in as a almost of counsel  if we're a law firm you know some some  you know some people there called me the  Mad Men on the hill because I had all  these sort of crazy ideas but the show I  think was very much about the government  we wish we had you know how we wish it  worked and that the ideals not perfect  but these men and women you know always  had a personal reason why they believed  or striving for for what they were  trying to achieve but it was in the best  sense as opposed to so much of what we  often feel and see which is the Craven  side of government I mean just look at  the  you know the number of people who are  indicted or heading toward jail or you  know it's it's uh you know the West Wing  was sort of the unfortunately not  necessarily real but in its own way  quite authentic version of what we wish  our government was and maybe what the  highest ideals of those who founded you  know who wrote the Declaration of  penance or the Constitution or you know  what they envisioned for America how did  you react to the criticism that the West  Wing was liberal well I mean it's one  what's wrong with taking a stand and  that doesn't mean you don't want to hear  other people disagree or argue with it  and the show had a sort of dance to it  that I thought was electric whatever the  the viewpoint so that it was at times  funny and it was a time smart hey bring  on those who are just as smart who have  a different point of view that's that's  great you know back to the book you the  impact on your family of this book being  written  what was it I think you know it's it's  twofold one is I think some really feel  it was a great thing to finally talk  about this it these things others are  still you know they are very much you  why did you you don't need to do that  you know we are from New England so we  maintain you know our version of silence  and and the other part is coming from a  large family is like when I showed to my  brother in manuscript he said you know I  Wow I think it's really good of course  it's not what happened because in a  large family you know I can remember  hearing in Massachusetts discussions  like how to get a certain place on Cape  Cod and the argument could go on for 45  minutes so the best way to get there you  know so you know anytime you have  several family members in a large family  you're gonna have a discussion about hey  I was there that's not what happened but  that's part of the book too is he's  learning that I was never gonna know  exactly what happened in every case and  I could only use sort of the limitations  of my memory and often that it was not  me discovering what happened but me  wishing I knew more you know that I wish  I knew more about my  I wish I knew more about my uncle and  that one of the things we don't usually  ask is enough questions before those who  come before us die what about the female  side of the equation is your mother  alive when you wrote this says it's  beautifully written and wish wish that  probably I didn't talk about things  certain things so much you know and  Doug's mother is still alive and she  would probably say it exactly the same  you know your you know why do you want  to stir things up again it would be part  of her response I think for me it was I  think also been honoring Doug you know  don't want I think much of what I do is  to say let's honor veterans I mean  veterans can be as whiny and is crazy in  a certain sense as anybody on the other  hand they have done something and served  and we have to honor them so honoring  Doug and also honoring I hope my father  my uncle and what about you did you  serve in the military no and I think  that's probably one of the reasons I  kept flunking physical for a stupid you  know football injuries and I think  that's one reason that I and not I alone  it's sort of but if Vietnam was sort of  the crucial event and issue of my  generation  John sacred yang is 